      {
        "id": "p50-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the derivative of $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{x^2-4}$ is $f'(x)=-\\dfrac{x^2+4}{(x^2-4)^2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-4-2x^2}{(x^2-4)^2}=-\\dfrac{x^2+4}{(x^2-4)^2}$.",
          "worked": "Using the quotient rule,\n$$\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{(x^2-4)(1)-x(2x)}{(x^2-4)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{x^2-4-2x^2}{(x^2-4)^2}\\\\&=-\\frac{x^2+4}{(x^2-4)^2}.\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain why the curve $y=f(x)$ has no stationary points, and why the curve is decreasing on each interval of its domain.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)<0$ throughout the domain, so there are no stationary points and the curve decreases on $(-\\infty,-2)$, $(-2,2)$ and $(2,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "For every $x$ in the domain, $x^2+4>0$ and $(x^2-4)^2>0$. Hence $f'(x)<0$ and never equals zero. There are no stationary points, and the curve is decreasing on each of $(-\\infty,-2)$, $(-2,2)$ and $(2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q14-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Given that $f''(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+24x}{(x^2-4)^3}$, show that $(0,0)$ is a point of inflection. Then find the gradient of the tangent at this point.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Concavity changes from up to down at $(0,0)$. The tangent gradient is $-\\dfrac14$.",
