      {
        "id": "p50-q14-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "State the domain of the function and the equations of any vertical asymptotes.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Domain: $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-2,2\\}$. Vertical asymptotes: $x=-2$ and $x=2$.",
          "worked": "$x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2)$ vanishes at $x=\\pm2$, where the numerator is nonzero. Thus the domain is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-2,2\\}$, with vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q14-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "What value does $f(x)$ approach as $x$ becomes large? Hence write down the equation of the horizontal asymptote.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)\\to0$; horizontal asymptote $y=0$.",
          "worked": "$$f(x)=\\frac{1/x}{1-4/x^2}.$$ As $x\\to\\infty$, $\\frac{1}{x}\\to0$ and $\\frac{4}{x^2}\\to0$, so $f(x)\\to0$. The horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 11
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q14-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Show that the function is odd. What symmetry does its graph have?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(-x)=-f(x)$; $180^\\circ$ rotational symmetry about the origin.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}f(-x)&=\\frac{-x}{(-x)^2-4}\\\\&=-\\frac{x}{x^2-4}\\\\&=-f(x).\\end{aligned}$$ Hence $f$ is odd, and its graph has $180^\\circ$ rotational symmetry about the origin.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q14-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "Analyse the behaviour of the function as it approaches the vertical asymptotes.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-2^-}f(x)=-\\infty$, $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-2^+}f(x)=+\\infty$, $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^-}f(x)=-\\infty$, $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)=+\\infty$.",
          "worked": "Using $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{(x-2)(x+2)}$: near $-2$ the numerator is negative; near $2$ it is positive. The denominator approaches $0^+$ from outside $[-2,2]$ and $0^-$ from inside.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to-2^-}f(x)&=-\\infty,\\\\\\lim_{x\\to-2^+}f(x)&=+\\infty,\\\\\\lim_{x\\to2^-}f(x)&=-\\infty,\\\\\\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)&=+\\infty.\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q14-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$, showing all important features.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Three decreasing branches, vertical asymptotes $x=\\pm2$, horizontal asymptote $y=0$, and an inflection point and intercept at $(0,0)$.",
          "worked": "Draw the asymptotes $x=-2$, $x=2$ and $y=0$. Draw a decreasing branch on each domain interval, using the one-sided behaviour in part g. The middle branch passes through the inflection point $(0,0)$ with gradient $-\\tfrac14$. The graph has rotational symmetry about the origin. As $x\\to-\\infty$, $f(x)\\to0^-$; as $x\\to\\infty$, $f(x)\\to0^+$.",
